// **What a `@derive(<BuiltinTrait>, via: f)` demands of its field is refused at the derive**, not
// at the first call.
//
// A `via:` derive registers membership whatever the field is, so a field that cannot answer the
// forwarded construct produces a type reporting the trait, satisfying `is dyn Trait`, and aborting
// on the trait's one method. The demand is therefore the same question the forwarded construct
// itself asks — `Comparable` an ordering, `Error` an `impl Error`, and the operator traits their
// own operator on the field — and every delegable built-in that has one states it here, so a trait
// gaining a template cannot arrive with the check missing.
//
// `Equatable` and `Display` are absent on purpose and are covered positively in
// `derive_via_builtin_doors`: `==` is universal and the `Display` template renders the field
// instead of calling a method on it, so neither can be handed a field it cannot use.
//
// Pinned at the **via field's span**, which only a static rejection reports: the run-time failure
// each of these produces without the check lands inside the synthesized method, at the `@derive`
// attribute's own span on the line above.
// expect: error E0050 at 26:26
// expect: error E0050 at 29:21
// expect: error E0050 at 32:19
// expect: error E0050 at 35:19
// expect: error E0050 at 38:19
// expect: error E0050 at 41:19
// expect: error E0050 at 44:22
// expect: exit 1
@derive(Comparable, via: xs)
struct Ordered { xs: List<int> }

@derive(Error, via: s)
struct Wrapped { s: string }

@derive(Add, via: s)
struct Summed { s: string }

@derive(Sub, via: s)
struct Taken { s: string }

@derive(Mul, via: s)
struct Scaled { s: string }

@derive(Div, via: s)
struct Split { s: string }

@derive(Concat, via: n)
struct Joined { n: int }

echo 0
